AB  - Standard confusion-matrix metrics (e.g., AUROC, Youden's index, F1, Jaccard) often do not explicitly account for class imbalance, asymmetric utilities, or performance relative to a no-information baseline. These limitations can obscure the utility structure relevant to model choice. We propose a unified utility-weighted evaluation framework for binary classification and information retrieval, together with a kappa-like chance correction. For classification, we define the utility-weighted accuracy rate (UWAR) by normalizing expected utility to the perfect-model utility, yielding a weighted average of sensitivity and specificity controlled by a single utility-weighting parameter that combines positive-class prevalence with the relative benefit of a true positive compared with a true negative. We then introduce the chance-corrected UWAR (ccUWAR) to quantify utility-weighted performance above the no-information baseline, with interpretable special cases that connect to familiar indices such as AUROC and Youden's index. For information retrieval, we develop parallel measures in the positivity-conditioned space: the conditional utility-weighted accuracy rate (CUWAR) and its chance-corrected version (ccCUWAR), parameterized by the relative cost of missed relevant items versus retrieved non-relevant items and the prevalence of relevant items; CUWAR reduces to the Jaccard index and is directly related to the F1 score when these error costs are equal. We further derive better-than-chance regions, indifference points, and regions of dominance. Through controlled analytical examples, we illustrate that rankings that appear fixed under symmetric metrics can reverse once utility structure and chance correction are made explicit. The framework is intended as a conceptual and analytical tool for utility-aligned evaluation rather than as a universal replacement for existing metrics. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
