AD  - Research Center of Information Security and Intelligent Technology, Harbin University of Science and Technology, Harbin, 150080, China
AB  - Federated learning (FL) is a privacy-preserving distributed machine learning paradigm that enables collaborative model training through local updates and model sharing. However, in realistic deployments the data are typically non-independent and identically distributed (non-IID), and in particular label shift severely limits the generalization ability and convergence efficiency of the global model. Existing model-averaging-based algorithms struggle in such heterogeneous settings, making them insufficient for many practical applications. To address these issues, we propose FedERFT, a federated learning framework with server-side staged optimization, which systematically improves model stability and generalization by combining feature generation with progressive fine-tuning. In the first stage, the server aggregates client models to obtain an initial global model. In the second stage, the global encoder is frozen and only the classification head is fine-tuned, allowing rapid adaptation to the feature distributions of different clients. In the final stage, both the global encoder and the classification head are jointly fine-tuned to further enhance overall performance. In addition, we design a feature generation strategy based on bidirectional Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence minimization. This strategy applies random dropout and rescaling to client parameter increments to generate diversified feature representations, effectively aligning client feature distributions and substantially mitigating the training instability caused by data heterogeneity. Experiments on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and Tiny-ImageNet show that FedERFT achieves a 1.5%-4.5% accuracy improvement across various non-IID scenarios, and exhibits particularly strong generalization and cross-client robustness under extreme heterogeneity. These results demonstrate that FedERFT provides an efficient, robust, and widely applicable solution for federated learning in non-IID environments. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
