AB  - Predicting the performance degradation of composite materials is of paramount importance for ensuring the safe operation of composite engineering structures. Traditional experimental methods involve protracted, costly accelerated ageing tests, while theoretical methods also subject to their own inherent limitations. Physics-based methods face parameter-tuning difficulties and incomplete degradation descriptions, while data-driven approaches are data-dependent and may have low accuracy. To overcome these challenges, this paper presents an innovative methodology that integrates interval degradation progress models with Bi-LSTM (Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory) neural networks to enhance prediction performance. Specifically, the Grey System Theory (GST) is employed to quantify the interval process. By combining GST with physical information, a novel quantification method for the interval degradation process model is proposed. This model serves as a reference framework, enabling the mapping of experimental degradation data. The Bi-LSTM network then harnesses this nonlinear mapping to forecast the degradation trajectory of composite materials, leveraging its powerful sequential learning capabilities. The accuracy of the proposed method is verified using two different experimental datasets: stiffness degradation under hygrothermal conditions and fatigue damage under cyclic stress. The results clearly demonstrate that this fusion approach outperforms existing methods in terms of predictive accuracy, indicating its great potential for practical applications in the field of composite material performance degradation prediction. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
