AB  - Nanoplastics are increasingly recognized as emerging stressors that impair photosynthesis in photosynthetic organisms, and their co-occurrence with environmental pollutants can further intensify phytotoxic effects. To address the fragmented understanding of these combined impacts, we integrated a meta-analysis of 1067 experimental observations with explainable machine learning to quantify how nanoplastics and co-pollutants jointly influence photosynthetic processes. The results showed predominantly negative effects across light reactions and rubisco enzyme activity, with organism species, nanoplastics dose, size, and exposure duration identified as major determinants of photosynthetic inhibition. High-sensitivity indicators of photosynthetic stress were identified, and corn emerged as a particularly sensitive organism. By linking physicochemical properties and exposure conditions to quantitative photosynthetic outcomes, this study provides a clearer and more accessible framework for predicting phytotoxicity and supports improved ecological risk assessment and management strategies. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
