AD  - College of Mechanical Engineering, Xinjiang University, Urumqi, 830017, China
AB  - High-entropy alloys (HEAs) have become a revolutionary breakthrough in the field of materials design due to their excellent mechanical properties, thermal stability, and corrosion resistance. However, their large compositional space poses a significant challenge to traditional design paradigms. This paper elucidates the fundamentals of machine learning (ML) -assisted HEAs design from a synergistic perspective integrating intrinsic HEAs mechanisms and ML methodology, systematically reviewing progress in ML application for HEAs research with a focus on three pivotal aspects: phase structure prediction, performance optimization, and critical bottleneck breakthrough. This review demonstrates that the deep integration of ML and HEAs research enables efficient construction of quantitative “component-structure-property” correlations while exhibiting significant advantages in mitigating data scarcity and enhancing model interpretability. Nevertheless, key challenges persist—model opacity, experimental validation delays, and multi-objective optimization complexity. Future research should prioritize developing inverse material design frameworks, establishing uncertainty quantification methods, and constructing digital twin validation platforms to bridge computational prediction and experimental verification. The deep fusion of data-driven techniques with thermodynamic parameters, material intrinsic features, and engineering process parameters will propel HEAs into a performance-driven intelligent customization era, delivering breakthrough solutions for biomedical, energy, and aerospace applications. © 2025
