AB  - With the increasing proportion of external power supply and the high penetration of renewable energy in Shanghai's power system, the demand for flexibility resources in urban grids continues to grow. Novel energy storage systems (NESS) feature fast response and high precision; however, systematic evaluation methods for their adjustable capability across diverse scenarios, such as peak-shaving and frequency regulation, are currently lacking. To address this gap, this paper proposes a flexibility demand quantification method based on load variation to characterize the grid's requirements. On this basis, an operational and economic model for energy storage is established, incorporating round-trip efficiency and battery degradation costs. Furthermore, a multi-dimensional evaluation framework is constructed, encompassing energy-oriented, power/ramp-rateoriented, and economic indicators to enable standardized scoring and classification of various configurations. Using a 50 MW/200 MWh energy storage station as a case study, simulation results demonstrate that the integration of energy storage significantly reduces the peak-valley difference and ramp rate of the urban grid, effectively meeting flexibility requirements. These findings provide a robust foundation for optimizing energy storage configurations and assessing adjustable capabilities in different application contexts.  © 2026 IEEE.
