TI  - CSNF-TimeGAN: Class-prior spatiotemporal nonlinear feature-based time series generative adversarial network for blast furnace fault diagnosis
PY  - 2026
T2  - Advanced Engineering Informatics
VL  - 71
C7  - 104213
DO  - 10.1016/j.aei.2025.104213
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105024855258?origin=resultslist
AD  - State Key Laboratory of Industrial Control Technology (ICT), Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310027, China
AB  - Fault diagnosis for the blast furnace ironmaking process (BFIP) is vital to ensuring production stability and efficiency. However, it is challenged by the complex interplay of nonlinearity, dynamic behavior, spatial correlations, and a significant scarcity of labeled fault data. To overcome these limitations, a novel fault diagnosis method based on class-prior spatiotemporal nonlinear feature-based time series generative adversarial network (CSNF-TimeGAN) is proposed. First, we develop the class-prior spatial improved mixed kernel canonical variate analysis (CSI-MKCVA) to extract class-prior spatiotemporal nonlinear features (CSNF). Unlike existing kernel CVA variants, CSI-MKCVA introduces a novel mixed kernel architecture integrating radial basis, polynomial, and cosine kernels to capture local, global, and temporally correlated nonlinearities in a unified manner. The architecture is further enhanced by incorporating class-prior information and Laplacian regularization into covariance estimation, preserving the spatiotemporal dynamics while maximizing fault separability. An efficient alternating Lagrangian optimization algorithm ensures robust convergence. Second, CSNF-TimeGAN is proposed as a diagnosis-oriented generative framework that bridges advanced feature representation and synthetic data generation. Operating a Wasserstein GAN with gradient penalty in the CSNF space, the model achieves stable distribution matching. It innovates theoretically through a temporal consistency loss and an adaptive loss informed by classifier feedback, as formalized in new equations that mitigate mode collapse and enhance discriminability beyond standard GANs. Subsequently, an integrated fault diagnosis mechanism is designed, synergizing feature representation, data generation, and classification into a cohesive workflow. In deployment, real-time diagnosis leverages the unified CSNF feature extractor and optimized classifier, ensuring computational efficiency. Finally, CSNF-TimeGAN demonstrates leading-edge performance through validation on real BFIP operational data. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.
