AB  - Context or problem: Balancing wheat production with improved nitrogen (N) use efficiency and reduced N loss depends on a clear understanding of fertilizer-N fate. However, integrative syntheses of 15N evidence across China's diverse wheat-growing regions remain scarce. Objective and methods: Using a compiled database of 421 observations from 86 published 15N field tracer studies in China's wheat systems, we integrated meta-analysis with machine learning to quantify the effects of climate, soil, and N management factors on wheat yield and 15N fate, and to predict region-specific attainable 15N uptake potential. Results and conclusions: Wheat yield under N fertilization (52.5–489.0 kg N ha-¹) averaged 6874.02 kg ha−1 nationwide, with fertilizer-15N exhibiting uptake, residual, and loss percentages of 37.29%, 31.53%, and 31.35%, respectively. Regionally, wheat yield ranked as Central China (CC) > Southern China (SC) > Northern China (NC), whereas fertilizer-15N uptake% ranked as CC > NC > SC. Random forest (RF) analysis showed different dominant drivers for yield (SOM, MAT, and BD) and fertilizer-15N uptake% (TN, MAP, and soil pH), suggesting that the inconsistent regional patterns between yield and fertilizer-15N uptake% may be partly attributable to differences in soil fertility and hydroclimatic conditions. Spatial prediction was used to estimate attainable fertilizer-15N uptake, defined as RF-predicted 15N uptake% based on gridded predictors representing broad spatial gradients. Regional attainable uptake was 46.76%, 51.67%, and 49.86% in NC, CC, and SC, respectively, with attainable–observed fertilizer-15N uptake differences of 10.24, 12.36, and 17.45 %age points. Implications: These findings provide a scientific basis for region-specific optimization of N management to enhance 15N uptake under local climatic and soil conditions, supporting sustainable precision agriculture. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
