TI  - Low-Resource Text Processing with Lightweight Multi-modal Agents: Enhanced Entity Recognition based on Few-Shot Learning
PY  - 2026
T2  - International Conference on Intelligent and Sustainable AI Systems, ICOSAAS 2026 - Conference Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/ICOSAAS68663.2026.11649291
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048464393?origin=resultslist
AD  - Taren Times Technology Group Co., Ltd., Beijing, 100098, China
AB  - In the context of low-resource text processing, the scarcity of labeled samples, insufficient semantic expression capabilities, and limited model knowledge calibration abilities are common practical challenges. The named entity recognition task generally faces issues such as decreased generalization performance and insufficient prediction stability. To address this core problem, this paper proposes a lightweight multimodal intelligent entity recognition framework for low-resource text processing. This method first constructs a multi-scale semantic plane representation mechanism, extending the traditional one-dimensional sequence encoding to a two-dimensional span-level semantic modeling structure. This paradigm can enhance the model's ability to express entity boundaries and context-dependent relationships. On this basis, this paper designs the Adaptive Biaffine Span Synthesis (ABSS) algorithm. This algorithm achieves context information fusion through multi-scale expansion convolution and dynamically estimates the importance of different semantic scales using the Scale-Gating Calibration Network (SGCN). Subsequently, this paper proposes a few-shot knowledge injection mechanism based on the Label Semantic Field (LSF). This mechanism realizes dynamic semantic alignment between text span representation and label knowledge by continuously modeling the label description information. At the same time, it enhances the classification ability through the Contrast-Calibrated Interaction (CCI) function. To solve the conflict between internal parameter knowledge and external context knowledge in low-resource environments, this paper constructs a lightweight knowledge reasoning intelligent agent. This paradigm introduces a knowledge conflict quantification method based on Jensen-Shannon divergence and an adaptive uncertainty decision-making mechanism based on information gain. This paper conducted systematic experiments on datasets such as Few-NERD INTRA, Few-NERD INTER, MIT-Restaurant Low-Resource, and a self-built dataset. The experimental results showed that in the 1-shot task of Few-NERD INTER, the method proposed in this paper achieved an F1 value of 42.05%. This result was 6.43 percentage points higher than the optimal baseline AugGPT-NER. Under the conditions of 5-shot and 10-shot, the F1 performance reached 53.96% and 62.38% respectively. On the noisy SME dataset, the method proposed in this paper outperformed StructShot by 8.09 percentage points in the 1-shot scenario. The ablation experiment results indicated that the model performance decreased by 7.75 percentage points after removing the label semantic field module. Meanwhile, the knowledge conflict analysis results showed that the uncertainty-aware reasoning mechanism proposed in this paper could significantly improve the recognition stability of high-conflict samples. © 2026 IEEE.
