AB  - Phishing is also a significant issue in cybersecurity, and it needs innovative solutions to make it detected and prevented instantly. The development of email protection infrastructure with the help of ML and DL algorithms has taken the lead in combating phishing. This paper proposes an effective phishing email detecting system based on the DistilBERT-base-uncased model, which is a more computationally efficient version of BERT, yet highly accurate. The Kaggle phishing email data set has undergone several operations including cleaning, one-hot encoding and TF-IDF vectorization and feature scaling. The data was balanced to address the imbalance of classes prior to the model training. The proposed model has already shown important results with a testing accuracy of 98.45, precision of 97.67, recall of 100 and F1score of 98.61, which are superior to already available traditional ML (DT, NB, LR, RF, AdaBoost, NB+KNN) and DL (CNN, LSTM, NN, MLP, RoBERTA) models. These findings indicate that the model is highly contextual, generalizable, and resistant to phishing detection errors. The research reveals that a lightweight transformer is able to offer optimum performance in regards to accuracy and also is power efficient thereby making possibility of scalable and real-time e-mail security solutions. © 2026 IEEE.
