AB  - Second-coordination-shell effects have emerged as a crucial factor in regulating the performance of single-atom catalysts for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), but quantifying their impact remains challenging due to their spatial distribution and lack of a unique descriptor. Here, we propose a feature-center-based spatial–physicochemical quantification method to describe second-coordination-shell effects in terms of composition and effective distribution, using a carbene-coordinated single-atom catalyst platform with varying ratios of N-heterocyclic carbene (NHC) and cyclic (alkyl)(amino)carbene (CAAC) ligands. High-throughput screening of 203 designed systems identified 32 computationally screened candidate systems, four of which exhibited low calculated limiting potentials of − 0.15 to − 0.17 V. Machine-learning analysis identified the first-ionization-energy feature-center-based descriptor (dIE) as an important catalyst-intrinsic descriptor for quantifying second-coordination-shell effects, highlighting the relevance of spatially distributed electronic characteristics to catalytic performance. The proposed representation further shows transferability to chemically related coordination environments. These results demonstrate the potential of the proposed framework for quantifying second-coordination-shell effects in carbene-coordinated single-atom catalysts and provide insights into the design of coordination-sensitive electrocatalysts. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
