AB  - This paper presents a streamlined and interpretable framework for handwritten digit classification on the MNIST dataset. While deep learning models offer superior accuracy, they often function as 'black boxes' with high computational demands. To address this, we propose a modular pipeline that integrates Principal Component Analysis (PCA) as a denoising filter with mutual information-based pixel selection and nonparametric kernel density estimation (KDE). Crucially, we find ICA to be redundant within reconstruction-based pipelines, as PCA denoising provides sufficient feature preservation for KDE modeling. The framework further incorporates an optimized Jensen-Shannon divergence-based weighting scheme to resolve confusing digit pairs and a Bayesian MAP decision rule using empirical class priors. The resulting method achieves an accuracy of (83.36%) while maintaining full pixel-level transparency and high computational efficiency. This work demonstrates that efficient generative models can provide a rigorous and explainable alternative to discriminative models in scenarios where interpretability and uncertainty quantification are paramount. © 2026 IEEE.
