AD  - School of Aeronautics and Astronautics, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Chengdu, 611731, China
AB  - Multimodal ship detection using optical and synthetic aperture radar (SAR) imagery is highly desirable for robust maritime monitoring. However, most existing fusion-based methods assume spatially aligned optical–SAR pairs, an assumption that rarely holds in practice due to differences in sensor geometry, acquisition timing, and registration errors. Such misalignment severely undermines the effectiveness of conventional fusion strategies. To address this challenge, we propose MHFNet, an alignment-aware hybrid fusion framework that systematically integrates improvements at the feature, loss, and decision levels. First, we introduce a Feature Alignment and Fusion Module (FAFM) that leverages deformable convolution and content-guided attention to achieve robust cross-modal feature alignment. Second, we design an Uncertainty-Aware Shape IoU Loss (US-Loss) that downweights unreliable training samples caused by cross-modal label shift while enhancing bounding box regression with shape-aware constraints. Third, we develop a Distance Matching Probabilistic Fusion (DMP-Fusion) algorithm that resolves inconsistencies in multimodal detection outputs through distance-based matching and Bayesian inference. Extensive experiments conducted on two benchmark datasets (QXS and Suez) show that MHFNet consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods, improving average precision by up to + 4.77 % and F1-score by + 4.93 %. These results demonstrate that MHFNet offers a robust and generalizable solution to multimodal ship detection under realistic cross-modal misalignment conditions. The code and datasets are released on https://github.com/wsdmw1/MHFNet. © 2025 International Society for Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing, Inc. (ISPRS)
