AD  - Department of Chemical and Petroleum Engineering, Sharif University of Technology (SUT), Tehran, Iran
AB  - Understanding wettability behavior in brine–CO2–mineral systems is fundamental to predicting the efficiency and long-term security of geological carbon storage. In this study, we combine statistical characterization, mechanistic interpretation, and advanced machine learning to quantify the thermodynamic, geochemical, and mineralogical controls on contact angle variability. Exploratory analyses reveal strong mineral-specific heterogeneity, with quartz and feldspar exhibiting predominantly hydrophilic behavior, mica displaying intermediate tendencies, and calcite showing the largest variability, consistent with its sensitivity to geochemical reactions and surface chemistry. Pressure emerges as a dominant driver of wettability alteration, but its effect is nonlinear and mineral-dependent, with increasing pressure promoting CO2-wet tendencies in some regimes while showing weaker or even opposite trends in others due to coupled temperature, salinity, and mineralogical influences. Machine learning models, including ensemble learners and neural approaches, demonstrate strong predictive capacity for contact angle estimation (R2 up to 0.91), with stacking ensembles achieving the highest accuracy and most balanced residual patterns. Feature attribution using Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) consistently highlights contact angle at ambient condition (θ0) and pressure as the most influential predictors, while mineral type, temperature, and salinity exert complementary roles in shaping wettability outcomes. Importantly, SHAP interaction analyses reveal synergistic effects, whereby elevated temperature and salinity amplify the predictive importance of pressure, underscoring the nonlinear, coupled nature of wettability controls. These findings establish a robust, interpretable framework that integrates physicochemical mechanisms with interpretable machine learning, providing robust predictive insights into wettability evolution under reservoir-relevant conditions. The outcomes advance fundamental understanding of multiphase interfacial processes and offer practical tools for risk assessment, capacity estimation, and secure implementation of carbon storage strategies. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
