AD  - Guangzhou MINO Equipment Co., Ltd, Guangzhou, 510640, China
AD  - State Key Laboratory of Electromechanical Integrated Manufacturing of High-Performance Electronic Equipments, Xidian University, Xi'an, 710071, China
AB  - The application of transfer learning methods has effectively mitigated the challenges posed by operational condition variations in the fault diagnosis field. However, the assumption of label space consistency between the source and target domain has hindered the broader applicability of those methods. In industrial scenarios, the fault types across source and target domains often exhibit partial overlap, with potential emergence of novel, unknown fault classes in the target domain. This complex transfer scenario, characterized by both domain shift and label shift, defines the challenge of universal domain adaptation. To address this, we propose a universal domain adaptation diagnosis framework specifically designed for cross-domain identification of unknown faults unknown. The proposed method first employs the neighborhood invariance learning to optimize the separability of samples in the target domain. Then, a category prototype enhanced inter-domain mixup strategy generates pseudo-unknown samples, while an open-set classifier assigns the probabilities to unknown fault samples. Finally, a category consistency constraint is introduced to refine the decision boundary between known and unknown fault categories. Multiple comparison experiments were conducted on the SDUST dataset to evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed method in these extreme transfer scenarios. The experimental results demonstrate that the method proposed in this manuscript achieves superior performance compared to state-of-the-art universal domain adaptation methods. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
