TI  - Ensemble learning head-neck motion predictor with validation and application in cybersickness evaluation
PY  - 2026
T2  - Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence
VL  - 177
C7  - 114856
DO  - 10.1016/j.engappai.2026.114856
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035843484?origin=resultslist
AD  - State Key Laboratory of Advanced Design and Manufacturing Technology for Vehicle, Hunan University, Hunan, Changsha, 410082, China
AD  - Shenzhen Research Institute, Hunan University, Shenzhen, 518000, China
AB  - Despite growing concerns over cybersickness (CS) in virtual reality (VR) systems, effective methods for quantifying CS and evaluating VR scene comfort remain lacking. This study provided and validated a bio-inspired ensemble learning head-neck motion predictor (HMP) that simulated the human neurosensory-motor control loop through coordinating machine learning models. Building upon the HMP outputs, we developed and evaluated a cybersickness index (CSI) quantification method for VR scenes comfort assessment based on head-neck motion. Using motion data collected from thirty participants exposed to five representative VR scenes, the HMP was trained and evaluated with a subject-independent validation strategy to prevent participant data leakage, achieving a mean absolute error of 1.43 ± 0.59 (dimensionless average of angular and linear errors). CSI quantification effectively stratified scene discomfort levels, with VR scene 1 (CSI = 3.47 ± 0.82) inducing severe symptoms, in stark contrast to the optimal comfort offered by VR scene 5 (CSI = 0.36 ± 0.15). Crucially, spectral analysis identified 0.35 Hz as a biomechanical threshold, where sustained body sway frequencies above this value exponentially increased CS severity. Furthermore, the derived CS risk curve established quantitative design guidelines, proposing CSI <2.49 as a safety benchmark for nausea prevention. While these thresholds were empirically obtained from the tested participant group and VR content set, they serve as foundational references that can inform future validation and generalization efforts. Overall, the proposed framework provides a practical and scalable pathway for mitigating CS and enhancing human-machine interaction performance in VR systems. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
