TI  - BottleEdgeNet: A Calibrated Edge-Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) Framework for PET Bottle Quality Inspection in Deposit Return Schemes (DRS)
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Conference on Technologies for Sustainability, SusTech
IS  - 2026
DO  - 10.1109/SUSTECH67720.2026.11536266
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041690327?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Information Communication, Institute of Data Analysis and Informatics, Corvinus University of Budapest, Budapest, Hungary
AB  - Ensuring the cleanliness and structural integrity of PET bottles is a key requirement for the effective operation of Deposit Return Schemes (DRS) in circular economy frameworks. Traditional inspection systems and commercial Reverse Vending Machines (RVMs) often fail to detect subtle defects, leading to high False Acceptance and Rejection Rates. This paper presents BottleEdgeNet, a Calibrated Edge-Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) framework for PET bottle quality inspection that integrates EfficientNet-B0, ResNet-50, and a Google Teachable Machine backbone within a unified, low-power inference pipeline implemented in TensorFlow Lite (FP32/INT8) for CPU-oriented edge inference. The proposed system introduces a class-wise power reweighting calibration layer to stabilize confidence scores and mitigate label-shift effects under heterogeneous lighting and background conditions. A hybrid dataset comprising 7,849 training, 972 validation, and 972 testing images (plus 80 real-world samples) was used to evaluate performance across four operational classes: Clean Intact, Clean Damaged, Dirty Intact, and Dirty Damaged. Internal tests revealed that EfficientNetB0 (FP32) achieved the best accuracy (87.96%), outperforming ResNet-50 (84.47%) and Teachable Machine (67.45%), with negligible degradation after INT8 quantization. External validation confirmed EfficientNet-B0’s generalization stability (87.5%), while ResNet-50 prioritized contamination detection and Teachable Machine provided a lightweight, no-code alternative for rapid deployment. To enhance field robustness, improvements should focus on data diversity and controlled illumination rather than model complexity. We report operational decision metrics (FAR/FRR) under argmax decision logic to connect model-level classification behavior with accept/reject decisions. In addition, edge-oriented efficiency is discussed through proxy-based considerations across FP32 and INT8 inference. Together, these analyses strengthen the practical relevance of the proposed framework by linking accuracy and confidence stabilization to DRS throughput, quality control, and deployment feasibility. The results demonstrate that calibrated Edge-CNNs can enable accurate, efficient, and scalable inspection of returnable PET bottles, supporting sustainable and decentralized DRS operations. © 2026 IEEE.
