AD  - LRIT URAC 29, Faculty of Sciences, Mohammed V University, Rabat, Morocco
AD  - RIITM Research Group, ENSIAS, Mohammed V University, Rabat, Morocco
AB  - Background: Accurate assessment of lung involvement in coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) patients is crucial for clinical decision-making. Traditional semi-quantitative and manual methods, while commonly used, may lack objectivity and reproducibility. This study aims to evaluate the clinical utility of artificial intelligence (AI)-based automated quantification of lung lesions and compare its performance with conventional semi-quantitative and manual approaches. Methods: This retrospective cross-sectional study was conducted at CHU Ibn Sina in Rabat from March 17 to May 11, 2020, and included 106 patients diagnosed with COVID-19 pneumonia. All patients underwent chest computed tomography (CT) using Siemens Somatom Definition AS+ equipment. Lung involvement was assessed using three methods: semi-quantitative scoring (Q20 and Q24), manual visual estimation by radiologists, and AI-based automated quantification. Concordance between the methods was evaluated using Lin's concordance correlation coefficient. Analysis time for each method was compared using the Kruskal-Wallis test. Results: AI-based quantification showed strong agreement with the semi-quantitative method, with a Lin's concordance correlation coefficient of 0.9271 [95% confidence interval (CI): 0.8975-0.9484]. The average analysis time was 65±8 s for AI and 28±5 s for the semi-quantitative method (P<0.001). The agreement in lesion classification between radiologists and AI reached 88%, indicating the potential reliability of AI in supporting clinical interpretation. Conclusions: AI-based automated quantification methods demonstrate high concordance with conventional techniques and provide consistent, objective, and reproducible assessments of COVID-19-related lung damage. Although AI requires more time than semi-quantitative approaches, its accuracy and reliability suggest it could complement or even replace traditional methods in certain clinical settings. Further large-scale studies are recommended to confirm these findings and assess the long-term clinical utility of AI in radiological evaluation. © AME Publishing Company, Taiwan Society for Therapeutic Radiology and Oncology.
