AB  - The kappa coefficient and accuracy are widely utilised metrics for evaluating classification systems in machine learning and quality inspections. However, traditional kappa suffers a significant drawback: its value depends on class prevalence (the proportion of instances in each category), which can misrepresent a system's actual performance. Furthermore, relying solely on point estimates for both kappa and accuracy overlooks the crucial impact of sample size and estimation error. This paper addresses these critical limitations by providing a comprehensive and practical method for estimating the lower bound of the intrinsic kappa coefficient and accuracy, generalised to classification problems with any number of categories. Unlike its traditional counterpart, the “intrinsic” kappa is robust to sample prevalence, offering a more reliable measure of a classifier's inherent ability. We develop both exact and approximate methods for estimating these lower bounds. Our results demonstrate that the approximate methods yield estimations remarkably close to those from the exact method across a wide range of sample sizes and misclassified instances. This reliability is further validated through extensive Monte Carlo simulations and benchmarking on diverse machine learning datasets with varying degrees of class imbalance. These findings confirm the proposed framework's practical utility for providing a stable, uncertainty-aware performance measure in high-stakes applications. Real-life examples further illustrate the straightforward application of these robust estimation methods for practitioners. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
