AD  - School of Architecture, Southeast University, 2 Sipailou, Nanjing, 210096, China
AD  - School of Design, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, 200240, China
AB  - Rapid urbanization has intensified the urban heat island effect, exacerbating heatwaves and threatening public health and energy security. Most previous studies have focused on blue or green spaces separately, whereas the combined cooling effects of Blue-Green Spaces (BGSs) have not yet been fully researched. The role of their morphological structure and landscape configuration in regulating diurnal land surface temperature variations remains largely unexplored. Moreover, traditional studies have generally relied on linear models, whereas the relationship between BGS and land surface temperature is inherently nonlinear. Machine learning methods can efficiently capture these complex, nonlinear mechanisms, enabling more precise quantification. Therefore, this study employs machine learning to quantify the cooling effects of BGS in high-density urban areas of Nanjing. Results reveal significant differences between daytime and nighttime cooling. Large Core areas provide strong daytime cooling but may release heat at night, while high landscape connectivity enhances cooling potential across both periods. Interaction analyses show that, for a fixed patch size, increasing the Core area strengthens daytime cooling, and for a fixed core area, highly connected patches further amplify cooling effects. This study highlights the complex role of BGS in urban thermal regulation and its diurnal variations, revealing the synergistic cooling effects of BGS morphology and landscape configuration, and providing scientific guidance for urban planning and climate-adaptive city development. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
