TI  - Data driven and neural network based approaches for optimization of film cooling parameters in gas turbines—A Comprehensive Review, Challenges and Future Trends
PY  - 2026
T2  - Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence
VL  - 181
C7  - 115636
DO  - 10.1016/j.engappai.2026.115636
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044121411?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Aeronautical Engineering, MVJ College of Engineering, Whitefield, Karnataka, Bangalore, India
AD  - Energy Research Lab, Department of Mechanical Engineering, TKM College of Engineering, Kerala, Kollam, India
AB  - Artificial intelligence and machine learning approaches are widely adopted for the optimization of multiple input parameters of complex thermal systems. The film cooling (FC) performance of gas turbines depends on various flow, geometric, and evaluation parameters. The optimization of these parameters is vital for the efficient design of FC systems. In this study, various data-driven and neural network-based optimization approaches applied for the optimization of geometric and flow parameters in gas turbines were summarized in detail. This study comprehensively reviews 216 articles spanning from 2005 to early 2025. The optimization approaches were grouped into six categories: surrogate-based, machine learning-based, deep learning-based, Taguchi-based, multi-criteria decision-making-based, topology-based optimization, and other novel methods. Each optimization approach was elucidated extensively for its application to different gas turbine components, the predictive model used, input parameters, response parameter, generation of training samples, search algorithm, and factor level. The various parameters of the gas turbine FC used in the optimization are explained in detail. The uncertainty quantification method, uncertainty parameters, and distribution type applied to the gas turbine wall surface were compared and discussed in this study. In addition, the challenges involved in the implementation of optimization approaches and future research trends on gas turbine FC optimization are highlighted. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
