C7  - 111337
DO  - 10.1016/j.compeleceng.2026.111337
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043714340?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Computer Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Jamia Millia Islamia, Delhi, India
AB  - Breast cancer remains a leading cause of cancer-related morbidity among women worldwide, where early and reliable diagnosis is critical for improving clinical outcomes. This study proposes a hybrid classification framework integrating DenseNet201 for hierarchical feature extraction, a Quantum-Inspired Neural Stem Cell Optimization (QNSCO) algorithm for feature selection, and a Support Vector Machine (SVM) for final classification. The QNSCO algorithm reformulates classical stem cell optimization within a probabilistic amplitude-based representation to enhance exploration–exploitation balance in high-dimensional feature spaces. The framework was evaluated on the BreaKHis histopathological dataset across four magnification levels (40×, 100×, 200×, and 400×) under binary and multi-class settings using patient-wise stratified validation and repeated cross-validation protocols. The proposed DenseNet201–QNSCO–SVM model achieved classification accuracies of 99.88% (binary) and 99.01% (multi-class), while maintaining strict fold-level isolation to prevent information leakage. External validation under zero-shot and few-shot transfer settings further demonstrated stable generalization under magnification shifts and class prevalence variations. These results indicate that the proposed framework provides a reproducible and computationally structured optimization approach for histopathological image analysis, with potential applicability in computer-aided diagnostic research. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.
