UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105020914080?origin=resultslist
AD  - University of Alberta, Edmonton, AB, Canada
AD  - University of Texas at Dallas, Richardson, TX, United States
AB  - Inverse design poses challenges due to its nonlinear, high-dimensional, and often ill-posed nature. Machine learning has emerged as a promising alternative, offering cost-effective alternatives to computationally expensive models but can yield inconsistent or non-physical results without proper uncertainty quantification. This research introduces a novel framework that integrates uncertainty quantification into inverse design using sequential machine learning-based surrogate modeling. The framework operates through two key processes: a trainee model that identifies candidate design inputs expected to produce the desired outputs, and an assessor that applies conformal inference, guided by a user-defined confidence level, to evaluate and filter these candidates. By eliminating unreliable predictions, the framework delivers robust and accurate design solutions. Notably, the proposed approach is model-agnostic and does not rely on predefined prior distributions or model-specific likelihoods, distinguishing it from Bayesian-based uncertainty quantification methods. We validate the approach through one mathematical example and three engineering case studies. Results demonstrate that our method provides more robust and accurate solutions compared to conventional single-model approaches for inverse design. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.
