AD  - Department of Information Technology, Vignan’s Foundation for Science, Technology and Research, Guntur (Dt), Vadlamudi, Andhra Pradesh, India
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Vignan’s Foundation for Science, Technology and Research, Guntur (Dt), Vadlamudi, Andhra Pradesh, India
AB  - The task of extracting emotion from poetry data is one that has received little attention in the literature. Poetry has a complex structure and emotional richness, in contrast to prose data such as tweets, product reviews, emails, and chats. This disparity makes the prediction of emotions from the poetry a challenging endeavor. Many prior studies focus on capturing emotions derived from local relations while overlooking a poem’s global relations. This study introduces Poetic-Net, a multi-channel neural architecture for effective emotion recognition in poetry that leverages both structural and contextual insights. The model integrates Global Centrality Relation (GCR) features extracted via graph-based learning to quantify each word’s contribution to a poem’s central theme, and contextual features derived by passing BERT-pretrained embeddings through a sequence of Bi-LSTM, CNN, and Attention layers. By fusing GCR and deep contextual representations, Poetic-Net becomes a robust system that captures subtle and complex emotional patterns inherent in poetic text. Poetic-Net classifies poetry into five emotion classes viz. “Happy," "Fear," "Sad," "Angry," and “Surprise". Due to the unavailability of public datasets on emotion-annotated English poetry, an English poetry dataset ABIEMO (American, British, and Indian Poetry datasets for Emotion detection) is developed to evaluate the performance of the proposed model. The proposed Poetic-Net is evaluated with five-word representations (GloVe, FastText, Word2vec-CBOW, Word2vec-SkipGram, and BERT) against baseline classification models. The proposed Poetic-Net model outperformed all the baseline models with an accuracy of 92.89% as both global and contextual features contributed significantly in feature extraction for improved performance. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2026.
