AD  - State Key Laboratory of Water Engineering Ecology and Environment in Arid Area, Xi’an University of Technology, Xi’an, 710048, China
AB  - The failure of cascade dams is prone to triggering the superposition, transmission, and amplification of risks in the reservoir group of a river basin. Accurate assessment of the comprehensive risks of cascade reservoirs is of decisive significance for the safe operation of water conservancy systems and the protection of people’s lives and property. At present, the traditional comprehensive risk analysis methods based on flood disaster risk transmission have obvious limitations. They only focus on single risk factors like flooding, and the flood routing analysis mostly adopts empirical formulas, resulting in insufficient assessment accuracy. To address this issue, we propose a novel multi-source comprehensive risk quantification method for cascade dams integrating Monte Carlo simulation, the hydrodynamic model and Bayesian networks. Firstly, Monte Carlo simulations are applied to the hydrodynamic model simulated in HEC-RAS by combining with self-developed programs using Visual Basic for Applications. This application enables automated analysis of actual river flood routing, considering random flood frequencies and random initial reservoir water levels. Meanwhile, machine learning algorithms including N-M, Adam, and DE are employed to conduct inverse analysis of river roughness, which is determined as 0.0833. Then, based on the analysis of overtopping damage from flood evolution after dam failures in cascade reservoirs, we quantitatively analyze the inter-dam transmission risks by applying the theory of comprehensive influence coefficients. Further, we combine the risk analysis results of floods, earthquakes and landslides for each dam in the system, and use Bayesian networks to assess the failure risk of each individual dam. Finally, by integrating the transmission risk analysis, we develop a multi-source comprehensive risk analysis method for cascade reservoir systems.This method is applied to four cascade dams in the Dadu River Basin of Sichuan Province, China. The results show that the main cause of the single dam failure is the rare high-energy floods, and earthquakes have a significant impact on dam losses. The Changheba Dam has the highest reliability and can block risks, while the Huangjinping Dam and the Luding Dam require focused attention due to their relatively high comprehensive risks. We recommend prioritizing enhanced monitoring and structural reinforcement for the critical dams including Huangjinping and Luding. This method is expected to accurately quantify the risk transmission between dams in cascade reservoir groups under multi-disaster scenarios, thereby contributing to risk management and plan formulation for the water conservancy system. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
