AD  - Faculty of Computer Science and Information Technology, University of Malaya, Jalan Universiti, Kuala Lumpur, Wilayah Persekutuan Kuala Lumpur, 50603, Malaysia
AB  - Federated long-tailed learning is difficult because the model must learn from clients that are both heterogeneous and class-imbalanced. Tail classes may appear on only a few clients, and the prototypes used by generative augmentation can therefore be shifted by sample scarcity, label skew, and classifier bias. FedR2G addresses this problem by making the generation condition reliable before generating more tail samples. It first trains clients with a class-prior-scaled ETF directional classifier, which maintains a stable inter-class angular reference while strengthening weak tail-class responses. Clients then upload normalized class prototypes and local class counts. On the server, FedR2G uses these counts to calibrate latent class centers and performs tail-aware generation from the calibrated anchors. A latent consistency loss returns the public generative prior to client training. Experiments on CIFAR-10-LT, CIFAR-100-LT, and TinyImageNet-LT show consistent gains, especially in severe long-tailed settings. On CIFAR-100-LT with IF = 100, FedR2G reaches 37.64% Top-1 accuracy, improves Few-class accuracy by 3.28 percentage points over RUCR, and raises Macro-F1 by 2.69 percentage points. Ablations under a fixed StyleGAN-XL generator further show that the gains come from corrected semantic anchors and count-aware calibration, not only from the generator prior. FedR2G thus provides a practical way to connect geometric correction with generative rebalancing under federated long-tailed constraints. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
