AB  - Evaluating predictive reliability under temporally evolving data distributions remains a critical measurement challenge in real-world machine learning systems. In educational contexts, student populations change over time, introducing temporal class-prior drift and generalization instability that are often overlooked by conventional evaluation protocols based on random data splitting or cross-validation. This study proposes a measurement-oriented evaluation framework for assessing predictive reliability under forward cohort-based temporal conditions. The framework integrates discrimination performance, validation–test generalization gaps, and probability calibration to provide a deployment-aligned assessment of model robustness. The approach is demonstrated through sequential student retention prediction across thirteen semester-level stages using longitudinal academic data, comparing a sequential deep learning model (Long Short-Term Memory, LSTM) with non-sequential ensemble baselines (Random Forest and XGBoost). Empirical results reveal substantial temporal variability in predictive reliability. Intermediate prediction stages exhibit the largest generalization gaps, indicating reduced temporal generalization stability under forward cohort evaluation, while temporal class-prior evolution contributes to instability across cohorts. Calibration analysis further shows that probability reliability follows a stage-dependent pattern, deteriorating in intermediate stages and partially recovering in later stages, even when discrimination performance remains competitive. These findings demonstrate that predictive reliability in non-stationary environments cannot be adequately assessed using discrimination metrics alone. Instead, a multi-dimensional measurement framework is required to capture temporal generalization stability and calibration behavior, providing a more realistic evaluation of predictive models under deployment conditions. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
