AB  - While supervised classification is becoming increasingly used in sustainability development, the issue of prior probability shifts often occurs in these contexts and remains important to be addressed. Prior probability shift characterizes an evolution of the prior probabilities between test samples and the class proportions observed in training dataset. A robust approach to address this issue is the minimax regret strategy. The regret corresponds to the loss of performance associated with a classifier compared to the optimal performance reached by the Bayes classifier with respect to given prior probabilities. The minimax-regret strategy minimizes the maximum regret of a classifier that can be reached when prior probabilities shifts occur. This paper is devoted to learn a minimax regret classifier in a context of mixed attributes, that can be coupled with any pre-trained neural networks for sustainable classification tasks, and that can take into account given costs of classification errors provided by the experts of the application domain. We especially show that we can learn a minimax regret classifier by learning a usual minimax classifier when considering a specific loss function. Experiments on real databases for sustainability development illustrate the benefits of our proposed approach. © 2026 IEEE.
