AB  - The dryline is well understood to be an important mechanism for convection initiation in the U.S. Southern Great Plains (SGP) during its peak severe weather season (March–June). However, the role of the dryline in the severe climatology of the SGP has not yet been quantified. In this study, a machine learning front-identification model (FrontFinder AI) is used to construct a climatology of March–June 0000 UTC drylines over 26 years (1995–2020). Severe storm reports between 2100 and 0300 UTC within the expected region of dryline convection initiation are attributed to the dryline to determine the fraction of the SGP severe climatology associated with drylines. From southwest Texas through western Oklahoma, over 50% of March–June severe storm days are associated with a dryline. During May__the month with the most severe reports__drylines are associated with over 75% of severe thunderstorm days in the Texas Panhandle and northwest Oklahoma. Across the entire SGP, drylines are associated with approximately 41% of severe storm reports and 48% of significant severe storm reports. © 2026 American Meteorological Society.
