AD  - Utah State University, Mathematics & Statistics Department, Logan, United States
AD  - Rice University, Department of Statistics, Houston, United States
AD  - Intelligence And Space Research, Los Alamos National Laboratory, Los Alamos, United States
AB  - Hyperspectral imaging (HSI) is crucial for remote detection of various targets. However, detection becomes challenging when targets are obscured by other objects in the scene. To address this, we apply 3D scene understanding methods to create spatially-relevant representations of HSI data. 3D scene understanding with hyperspectral data has the ability to improve target characterization, as it allows for differentiation between materials that may appear similar in standard visual imagery and provide better spatial discrimination compared to single-image captures of a scene. Our approach enables the incorporation of multiple observations from different angles, enhancing the ability to 'look around' targets or obstructions by rendering new views of a scene. We apply the 3D methods of neural radiance fields (NeRFs) to Long Wave IR hyperspectral imagery. We will present results from our analysis pipeline applying machine learning 3D scene understanding methods to physics-driven synthetic images from DIRSIG to quantify accuracy tradeoffs with number of collected view angles, view geometry, and material composition of the scene. We demonstrate the capability of scaling the spectral dimensionality of NeRF models from data comprising single channel to multi-channel imagery, while also including uncertainty quantification methods to measure associated confidence in modeling spatial-spectral components of the scene. © 2026 IEEE.
