AB  - Multi source domain adaptation has recently emerged as an effective approach for well logging lithology identification under cross well distribution shifts. However, most existing approaches are built on the assumption that all source domains are fully labeled. In practice, source wells may be only partially annotated due to limited expert resources and operational constraints. Such partial annotation often leads to degraded performance, especially when the available labels are inconsistent or incomplete across different source wells. To tackle these challenges, we propose SR2-MSDA, a semi-supervised multi-source domain adaptation framework. SR2-MSDA employs a dynamic source weighting strategy that adaptively adjusts source domain weights based on their proximity to the target in feature space, and leverages unlabeled source samples through a teacher-student consistency scheme. In addition, to mitigate label heterogeneity across domains,we propose modulating the adversarial loss by integrating the target class prior with a class level gating mechanism for each source sample. Experiments on real well log data from an eastern oilfield show that SR2-MSDA consistently improves Accuracy and Macro-F1 by 4%-6% over multi source domain adaptation methods that utilize only labeled source samples.  © 2026 IEEE.
