AB  - With the advancement of computer image recognition technology, traditional exposure measurement methods urgently need improvements in effectiveness and measurement accuracy. This study employs a method based on image processing and computer vision technology, combined with a wearable device aiming to measure individual exposure to geographic environments. A spatiotemporal weighting algorithm was applied to dynamically adjust exposure data, and comparisons with traditional methods were conducted for validation. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method can accurately measure exposure across different environments, particularly achieving higher classification accuracy in natural and built environments. Compared with traditional questionnaire-based approaches and geographic information system methods, the proposed method exhibits significant advantages in real-time assessment, accuracy, and detail capture of exposure. In natural environments, the study revealed significantly higher exposure levels to green vegetation and water bodies compared to urban roads. In cultural environments, commercial areas showed higher exposure levels, reflecting the influence of population density and social activities. Additionally, the stability and applicability of the method in real-world scenarios were validated, consistently providing reliable measurement results across different geographical regions and environmental conditions. This study empirically demonstrates an innovative application of computer vision technology, with significant prospects for public health applications. It advances the value of big data in interdisciplinary research. © 2025 IOS Press.
