AD  - Research Center on Fictitious Economy and Data Science, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100190, China
AD  - Center for Applied Optimization, Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, University of Florida, Gainesville, 32611, United States
AB  - Positive-unlabeled (PU) learning trains binary classifiers using a limited subset of labeled positives alongside vast unlabeled data. Existing methods predominantly rely on pointwise supervision, thereby neglecting the rich latent supervisory signals inherent in the unlabeled data. To address this, we propose a novel paradigm that augments traditional instance-level supervision with a pairwise comparison (Pcomp) perspective. By constructing positive-unlabeled sample pairs, this paradigm learns ranking relationships where a labeled positive instance is expected to exhibit a higher positive confidence than an unlabeled one. This formulates PU learning as a Pcomp classification problem subject to one-sided noise from hidden positives. To render the Pcomp loss tractable under unknown class priors, we devise a Bayesian strategy that iteratively estimates the prior by explicitly modeling instance-dependent labeling probabilities. Furthermore, to mitigate the noise inherent in constructed sample pairs, we introduce a theoretically grounded loss decomposition mechanism. This strategy decouples the Pcomp loss into cyclically symmetric and asymmetric components, exploiting the intrinsic noise-invariance of the former while applying targeted corrections exclusively to the latter. Consequently, our method achieves strong robustness, even in label-scarce regimes. Extensive benchmark experiments demonstrate that our approach significantly outperforms state-of-the-art algorithms, substantiating both its effectiveness and robustness. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.
