AD  - Tianjin Key Laboratory of Urban Transport Emission Research & State Environmental Protection Key Laboratory of Urban Ambient Air Particulate Matter Pollution Prevention and Control, College of Environmental Science and Engineering, Nankai University, Tianjin, China
AB  - The combined strategy of vehicle electrification and traffic intelligence is a viable option for decarbonizing road transport and improving energy efficiency. However, the difficulty in quantifying the energy saving potential hinders the sustainability of this strategy. This study combines Markov chains and machine learning to quantify the energy saving potential of new energy vehicles (NEVs) under all‑mode Intelligent Transportation Speed Guidance System (ITSGS). The combined strategy of Multi-Intersection ITSGS (MITSGS) and NEVs (plug-in hybrid electric vehicle, PHEV and battery electric vehicle, BEV) has significant energy saving potentials of up to 21% and 79%, respectively, when compared to Internal Combustion Engine Vehicle (ICEV) without ITSGS. For the PHEV, the energy consumption model and reported results correspond to charge‑sustaining operation. The energy consumption of other combination strategies is as follows. When without ITSGS, the average energy consumption of ICEV, PHEV and BEV is 0.62 ± 0.07 kWh/km, 0.56 ± 0.05 kWh/km and 0.15 ± 0.01 kWh/km, respectively. When with single-intersection ITSGS (SITSGS), these values are 0.56 ± 0.04 kWh/km, 0.52 ± 0.04 kWh/km, 0.14 ± 0.01 kWh/km. When with MITSGS, these values are 0.54 ± 0.04 kWh/km, 0.49 ± 0.04 kWh/km, 0.13 ± 0.01 kWh/km. ITSGS reduces energy consumption by improving vehicle driving behavior at intersections. The framework harmonizes the energy consumption assessment scales of full-mode ITSGS and NEVs combination strategies, which is helpful for the promotion and popularization of combination strategies. This framework uniquely harmonizes scenario‑based driving cycles with machine learning to enable consistent, quantifiable evaluation of energy saving potentials across different ITSGS modes and vehicle types. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
