AB  - Accurate phenotypic quantification in poultry is essential for precision breeding and genetic improvement. Recent advances in deep learning have substantially improved the automation and accuracy of phenotyping. However, non-invasive and precise measurement of specific anatomical traits, particularly wings and feet, remains challenging. To address this challenge, we propose a CT imaging-based framework combined with deep learning to quantify wing and foot characteristics in live chickens. Specifically, we develop the DGCUNet for estimating the volumes of eight phenotypic targets associated with wings and feet. The encoder incorporates dilation transformer modules and stage-specific learning strategies to capture hierarchical representations, while the decoder employs a multi-scale fusion architecture to enhance feature integration. Four CT datasets covering multiple chicken breeds are constructed to validate the proposed framework. On the WB2024 dataset, five-fold cross-validation results demonstrate that DGCUNet achieves a Dice similarity coefficient of 86.41%, a 95th percentile Hausdorff distance of 3.28 mm, and an average symmetric surface distance of 0.69 mm. Furthermore, regression models establish relationships between segmented volumes and ground truth weights, and Bland-Altman analysis illustrates the agreement between estimated and actual weights. Overall, our framework enables fine-grained quantification of individual phenotypic traits rather than coarse whole-body measurements, demonstrating the potential of CT imaging and deep learning for advancing poultry phenotyping. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
