AB  - Many studies concerning the occurrence of MPs in urban areas so far are prone to a variety of uncertainties due to significant deficiencies in temporal/spatial resolution of sampling, representativeness of samples, sampled volume/mass adequacy, size classification, and characterization. Even more important, a remarkable lack exists in assessing the suitability of methods/concepts needed for quantification of MP fluxes. In this study, quantification methods based on mass loadings (flux) analysis rather than details on number and size/shape are suggested for the estimation of MPs occurrence and release from urban areas, which is considered one of the most significant sources of MPs in the environment. MP sources are categorized in different classes e.g.: houses/offices/workshops (textile washing, kitchen work, bathroom activities, municipal waste management, carwash) and traffic, which is mainly responsible for the generation of tire and road wear particles. Release of MPs considers various pathways such as compost/sludge, (un)treated wastewater/surface runoffs/baseflows and wind-driven transport. A conceptual model is proposed to allow quantification of total discharged MP mass flux in relevant pathways normalized by population density. This has the advantage of comparing MP fluxes with other relevant fluxes, such as particulate organic carbon or ubiquitous pollutants. A detailed classification of existing urban wastewater and solid waste collection and disposal networks is presented, which is a prerequisite for the potential implementation of improved waste management strategies. Finally, to achieve an unbiased/ fair share of different cities to global MPs generation, a scheme including factors of tourism intensity, economic well-being, and plastic waste trade is suggested. © University of Tehran 2026.
