AB  - The understanding of ozone (O3) formation processes in polluted industrial areas remains limited. A machine learning (ML) approach combining random forest (RF) and artificial neural network (ANN) algorithms was proposed, showing a strong predictive performance of 0.940 in R2. Analysis of variable importance suggests that NO2 concentration, temperature (T), and relative humidity (RH) are the most important variables. Focusing on prevalent conditions, we established a quantified function for predicting O3 concentration based on these important variables, which demonstrates a robust predictive capability. The results suggesting that the influence of NO2 concentration on O3 formation is primarily controlled near linearly by T and quadratically by RH. These results improve the understanding of O3 formation in those NOx-sensitive and VOC-oversaturated areas, thereby providing further support for identifying high O3 concentration periods and important influence variables, which help to mitigate the increase in O3 concentration in similar areas. Additionally, the RF-ANN model provided a deep understanding of O3 formation characteristics, while the quantification method with controlled variables demonstrated their effectiveness and timeliness in supporting local O3 concentration control strategies. © 2025 The Institution of Chemical Engineers
