AD  - Department of Biophysics, Kobe University Graduate School of Health Sciences, 7-10-2 Tomogaoka, Suma-ku, Hyogo, Kobe, 654-0142, Japan
AB  - Background and objective: Prior probability shift between training and deployment datasets can compromise deep learning-based medical image classification, and standard posterior reweighting is not uniformly beneficial. We developed a feature-separability-based reliability assessment framework to identify when prior correction is likely to improve pathological cell classification. Methods: We analysed 303 colorectal cancer specimens with CD103/CD8 dual immunostaining, generating 171,638 annotated cell images across 19 cell types. After class-capped, imbalance-reduced sampling, 16 cell types were evaluated using ResNet models trained under controlled prior-bias conditions. Feature separability was quantified using cosine similarity-based likelihood quality scores derived from intra-class and inter-class relationships in learned embedding spaces. Associations with F1 performance were examined together with prior-bias magnitude and sample adequacy. Results: Feature separability was the dominant predictor of F1 performance in the multivariable model (β=1.650, p<0.001), whereas prior-bias magnitude showed a much smaller effect (β=0.004, p=0.018). A likelihood-quality threshold of 0.294 provided an empirical operating point for identifying cases likely to benefit from correction. Overall, 78.0% of cases showed no improvement after correction, indicating that correction should not be applied uniformly. Supplementary ViT and UNI analyses showed the same broad separability-reliability relationship. Conclusion: Feature separability, rather than prior-bias magnitude alone, determined when prior correction improved classification performance. The proposed framework supports selective correction decisions during labelled validation, with task-specific thresholds requiring recalibration for other datasets. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.
