AB  - Background Cluster headache is a highly disabling trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia associated with substantial diagnostic delay, severe pain-related disability, and limited access to evidence-based treatments. In Brazil, epidemiological data are scarce, and CH is not formally recognized within rare-disease policy frameworks. Clarifying the available prevalence evidence may help guide surveillance, research priorities, and health-policy planning. Objective To analyze the prevalence of cluster headache (CH) in Brazil and assess whether the currently available Brazilian population-based evidence is compatible with national rare-disease criteria, while discussing clinical and public-health implications. Methods We conducted a systematic review of population-based epidemiological studies on CH in Brazilian adults, reported according to PRISMA 2020. Searches were performed in PubMed/MEDLINE, LILACS, and SciELO on March 1, 2026, covering publications from January 1, 2010, to March 1, 2026, with no language restrictions. Eligible studies were original Brazilian population-based studies in adults reporting CH prevalence or providing a denominator allowing prevalence estimation. Diagnosis was required to be based on ICHD-3, ICHD-3 beta, ICHD-2, or clearly described clinical criteria compatible with CH diagnosis. Brazilian Ministry of Health and CONITEC documents were reviewed to identify regulatory definitions and policy criteria for rare-disease classification. Methodological quality and risk of bias were assessed using the JBI Critical Appraisal Checklist for Studies Reporting Prevalence Data. Results The search identified 19 records. After removal of 12 duplicates, 7 records were screened, 4 full-text articles were assessed for eligibility, and 1 population-based Brazilian study met inclusion criteria. The eligible study, conducted in Barbacena, Minas Gerais, identified 15 CH cases among 36,145 adults, corresponding to a prevalence of 41.4 per 100,000 adults. This estimate is below the Brazilian rare-disease threshold of 65 per 100,000 inhabitants. The best available Brazilian population-based estimate is therefore compatible with CH meeting the Brazilian rare-disease prevalence threshold, although national confirmation requires additional population-based studies. Conclusion Current Brazilian population-based evidence is compatible with cluster headache meeting the national rare-disease prevalence threshold. However, this conclusion is based on a single population-based study, and further multicenter epidemiological research is required. Formal recognition may be scientifically justified and could support public-health planning, epidemiological surveillance, professional training, and development of national care pathways for diagnosis and treatment. © 2026, Brazilian Headache Society. All rights reserved.
