AB  - The broad learning system (BLS) is a shallow network architecture commonly adopted for classification and regression tasks. However, BLS and its variants struggle with imbalanced classification due to architectural redundancy from random feature projection and the lack of mechanisms for differentiating sample importance across classes, typically resulting in predictions biased toward majority classes. To overcome these challenges, we propose an elastic net-based cost-sensitive BLS with F-measure maximization and density harmonization (FDECBLS). This framework comprises two core innovations: (1) an elastic net regularization applied to singular values to mitigate redundancy and improve discriminative ability; (2) a dual-cost learning mechanism that combines a density-based cost scheme, which dynamically adjusts sample importance based on local neighborhood structures and global class imbalance information, with a penalty mechanism derived from F-measure maximization to impose class-specific costs for minority-class prioritization. The associated optimization problem is solved efficiently via the alternating direction method of multipliers, with theoretical convergence guarantees and empirically verified computational efficiency. Extensive experiments on 35 binary-class and 9 multi-class imbalanced datasets demonstrate the effectiveness and efficiency of FDECBLS. It attains leading average scores of 90.69% in F-measure, 92.77% in G-mean, and 93.24% in AUC on binary tasks, while consistently surpassing state-of-the-art baselines in multi-class settings. The code is available at https://github.com/AdamMing/FDECBLS. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
