AD  - School of Communications and Information Engineering, Xi'an University of Posts and Telecommunications, Xi'an, 710121, China
AB  - The minimum error thresholding (MET) method is based on the Bayes minimum classification error principle. Under different assumptions of the initial grayscale distribution of an image, MET criterion functions based on the mixed Gaussian distribution, mixed Poisson distribution, and shifted Rayleigh distribution have been proposed. Mathematical explanations have been presented for the MET criterion functions of mixed Gaussian and Poisson distributions from relative entropy and the joint maximum likelihood principle. However, the explanations for the MET criterion function of the shifted Rayleigh distribution (RMET) have not been presented. In this paper, the detailed joint maximum likelihood principle and relative entropy interpretations of the RMET method were presented, and the principles for calculating the class parameters in the RMET method were also explained. Moreover, since the class prior probability is unknown, it is more reasonable to use conditional likelihood estimation. Therefore, a new MET method of the shifted Rayleigh distribution is proposed based on the conditional maximum likelihood function. Performance tests on synthetic images, synthetic aperture radar (SAR) images, and natural images in the experiments confirm the effectiveness of the proposed new MET algorithm. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
