AB  - Precise estimation of CPU and memory usage in cloud settings is crucial for improving resource allocation efficiency and reducing operational expenditures. The proposed machine learning framework in this work aims at predicting average CPU usage and average memory usage from task-level metadata of the Google 2019 Cluster Dataset with features of scheduling class, priority, requested resources, task duration, and failure status. Hybrid models that integrate tree-based techniques (LightGBM, Random Forest) and deep learning models (Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Multilayer Perceptron (MLP)) are compared to isolated models such as XGBoost and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). Experiments prove that the hybrid LightGBM + MLP + LSTM model performs better with an RMSE of 0.08 for CPU prediction and 0.12 for memory consumption prediction and R2 scores of 0.94 and 0.91, respectively. Results indicate the importance of incorporating task metadata, especially resource requests and time, for enhancing scheduling efficiency. This eliminates provisioning errors by 25%, supports dynamic scaling of resources, and reduces infrastructure expenses in cloud data centers. © 2026 The Authors.
