AD  - Chengdu University of Technology, School of Mechanical and Electrical Engineering, Sichuan Province, Chengdu, 610059, China
AB  - Multimodal Sentiment Analysis (MSA) is a critical technology for human-machine interaction. It performs sentiment analysis by integrating complementary information from multiple modalities such as text, audio, and vision. However, most existing multimodal fusion methods regard the text as the primary modality and treat speech and visual modalities as auxiliary. This approach limits the potential for synergistic interaction among the three modalities. To address this limitation, this paper proposes a Dual-Channel Collaborative Transformer (DCCT), which enables collaborative interaction among the three modalities. DCCT incorporates a Self-modal Binding Channel (SBC) to enhance intra-modal consistency, and a Cross-modal Binding Channel (CBC) to complement the idiosyncratic features of other modalities. Then, a Feed-Forward Network (FFN) integrates the outputs of both channels for effective trimodal feature fusion. Furthermore, to mitigate the label shift problem in multi-task joint learning, we propose a framework that distinguishes between intensity and polarity tasks based on modality decomposition. By separating common and idiosyncratic features, we design dedicated tasks for intensity and polarity, and use a shared-private linear layer in the Trimodal Label Generator (TMLG) to capture the interrelation between the two aspects and facilitate task adaptation. Experiments conducted on the CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI datasets show that the proposed Dual-Stream Collaborative Network (DSCN), which integrates DCCT and TMLG, achieves ACC-2 accuracy of 86.75 % and 86.69 %, respectively. These results outperform the best existing baseline by 0.65 % and 0.83 %. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
