AB  - In the classical Dorfman group testing procedure Dorfman (1943), a fixed population of size N is divided into k pools of equal size n[jls-end-space/]. Each pool is tested: a negative result clears all n individuals, whereas a positive result triggers individual testing within the pool. Put (Formula presented) where p denotes the infection prevalence and qn is the probability that a pool is positive under perfect classification. The expected number of tests, denoted by XN[jls-end-space/], is given by (Formula presented) However, most applied presentations focus mainly on the expected number of tests and do not quantify the stochastic risk of exceeding a finite laboratory capacity when the number of pools, the total sample size, or the diagnostic accuracy is uncertain. This gap is important for operational screening programs, where a design that is optimal in expectation may still overload the laboratory with non-negligible probability. First, this paper investigates the distribution of XN[jls-end-space/], allowing us to derive the threshold probability (Formula presented) for exceeding a given testing capacity x[jls-end-space/]. Second, we further extend the model by considering random population sizes N and incorporating test misclassification through sensitivity (Formula presented) and specificity (Formula presented). In this setting, (Formula presented) We also add variance formulae, a normal tail approximation, a pool-size-dependent sensitivity (Formula presented), and a Monte Carlo validation showing that the analytical formulae match simulated operating characteristics. © 2026 Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/
