AB  - Public charging stations (PCS) have become critical infrastructure in urban transportation systems. PCS charging demand patterns are driven by complex mechanisms that conventional methods struggle to quantify, while the influence of the built environment (BE) remains underexplored. An integrated empirical framework is proposed that combines machine learning and causal inference models to analyze PCS demand patterns and generate evidence-based policy implications. Specifically, XGBoost with partial dependence plots is employed to predict demand, identify key determinants, and reveal nonlinear relationships, followed by double machine learning to quantify the causal effects of critical policy-relevant factors. We construct a real-world charging dataset incorporating BE, temporal, and price attributes for empirical analysis. Results show that charging demand is shaped by public transport accessibility and the spatial configuration of PCS. Electricity price has a stronger negative impact in areas farther away from subway stations. These findings support optimized charging infrastructure planning and targeted policy interventions. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
