AD  - Jiangsu Key Laboratory of Urban Underground Engineering and Environmental Safety, Institute of Geotechnical Engineering, Southeast University, Nanjing, 210096, China
AD  - School of Civil Engineering, Central South University, Changsha, 410075, China
AD  - National Engineering Research Center of High-Speed Railway Construction Technology, Changsha, 410004, China
AB  - Due to freeze–thaw cycles, rocks in cold regions are prone to fatigue damage, making the accurate prediction of their dynamic triaxial strength critical for mining engineering in cold regions. A physics-informed neural network (PINN) approach was proposed, integrating rock mechanics theory with neural networks to improve prediction accuracy, interpretability, and generalization. To enhance reliability, an uncertainty-aware PINN (UAPINN) model was developed by incorporating uncertainty quantification. To assess UAPINN model performance, six machine learning models were developed, and their performance was evaluated using metrics such as R2, MAE, RMSE and VAF. A feature importance quantification method based on Bayesian neural network uncertainty analysis was introduced. The results show that the UAPINN model outperforms traditional models, with the performance ranking: UAPINN > PINN > LightGBM > CatBoost > GRU > ELM > RF > SVR. Feature importance analysis reveals that the most influential parameters for dynamic triaxial strength are confining pressure > P-wave velocity > strain rate > freeze–thaw cycles > water absorption > dry density. This study provides an efficient method for predicting rock strength in cold-region engineering applications. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
