TI  - FedMCLA: Multi-Cluster Label-Shift-Aware Backdoor Defense for Heterogeneous IoT Federations
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Internet of Things Journal
DO  - 10.1109/JIOT.2026.3733041
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050237063?origin=resultslist
AD  - North China Electric Power University, Department of Foreign Studies, Hebei, Baoding, China
AD  - Hebei University, School of Cyber Security and Computer, Department of Computer Teaching, Hebei, Baoding, China
AD  - Hebei Finance University, School of Financial Technology, Hebei, Baoding, China
AB  - Federated intelligence enables heterogeneous Internet of Things (IoT) gateways to train without sharing raw data, but non-IID label skew lets malicious updates hide among overlapping benign modes. FedMCLA uses a client-specific label-shift signature to jointly drive fuzzy multi-reference consensus and client-conditioned benign-update reconstruction. The defense runs at a compute-capable aggregation coordinator with visible client updates. Vision models use their native classifier bias; the decoder-only federated large language model (FLLM) path requires a persistent vocabulary-sized output-bias augmentation, so it is not purely coordinator-side. FedMCLA is also incompatible with standard secure aggregation that reveals only an aggregate. Experiments cover vision and FLLM attacks, matched method-specific white-box attacks, contamination stressors, and deployment envelopes. Under matched defense-specific attacks on CIFAR-10, FedMCLA obtains 23.14% ASR, 63.36% benign accuracy (BA), and 7.53% benign error rate (BER), versus the next-lowest ASR of 42.68% for FedDLAD; with 50% same-round update exposure, Attack-MCLA+-E reaches 34.26% ASR. Across seven standard CIFAR-10 attacks, FedMCLA reduces mean ASR from 18.65% with DoBlock to 7.33%. On three Alpaca attacks, it achieves 9.12%–12.45% ASR while keeping MMLU within 1.2 percentage points of the undefended model. © 2014 IEEE.
