AB  - Ammonia nitrogen stress causes massive mortality in industrial aquaculture, making real-time monitoring critical. However, existing methods for recognizing fish stress behaviors often focus on state classification while neglecting the interpretability of behavioral response patterns. To address the above issue, an interpretable behavioral response analysis method based on the spatial distribution quantification of single-frame images was proposed. Cultured sturgeon under ammonia nitrogen stress were used as the experimental subject. The proposed method first constructed a lightweight object detection model, MD-YOLO, to precisely extract fish spatial positions and provide high-quality coordinate data for spatial distribution quantification. Subsequently, seven biologically meaningful quantitative indicators were constructed to characterize the spatial distribution of fish schools from three perspectives: population aggregation, spatial coverage, and distribution uniformity. Unlike end-to-end deep learning methods, these indicators quantitatively characterize the spatial behavioral patterns of stressed fish, thereby significantly improving the interpretability of behavioral analysis. By combining these indicators into a feature vector and utilizing a Random Forest (RF) classifier, accurate analysis of stress behavior was achieved. The experimental results showed that the MD-YOLO model outperformed the baseline model YOLOv10n by 1.1% and 4.4% in mAP@0.5 and mAP@0.75, respectively. Furthermore, the proposed method successfully characterized fish behavioral responses to ammonia nitrogen stress and achieved a recognition accuracy of 97.2%. The single-frame spatial distribution quantification method proposed in this study does not require cross-frame trajectory modeling, providing a high-precision, low-complexity, and interpretable solution for fish behavior monitoring in industrial aquaculture. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
