AB  - The RHT constitutive model has been widely applied in numerical simulations of rock blasting. However, a clear quantitative relationship between its damage variable D and macroscopic fragmentation degree is still lacking. In this study, single-hole blasting experiments were combined with LS-DYNA simulations to investigate crater morphology, fragment size distribution, and damage evolution. A damage quantification method based on damaged volume ratio was proposed, establishing a direct quantitative correlation between the RHT damage variable D and fragmentation degree. Based on this relationship, blast-induced damage is classified into four levels: undamaged/slight damage (0 ' D ≤ 0.3), moderate damage (0.3 ' D ≤ 0.8), severe damage (0.8 ' D ' 0.9), and crushed damage (0.9 ≤ D ≤ 1). The proposed damage–fragmentation correlation provides a quantitative reference for interpreting numerical damage fields and offers practical guidance for improving blasting damage prediction and fragmentation control in numerical simulations. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.
