TI  - The Youth Online Exploitation–Extremism Interface: A Conceptual Framework for Anti-Trafficking Identification and Digital Child Protection
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Human Trafficking
DO  - 10.1080/23322705.2026.2725237
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048868846?origin=resultslist
AD  - College of Psychology and Community Services, Walden University, Minneapolis, MN, United States
AB  - Technology-facilitated child sexual exploitation increasingly intersects with coercive digital abuse and violent online subcultures that reward humiliation, self-harm, and abuse-material production as status markers. Conventional anti-trafficking identification is poorly suited to these cases, where exploitation operates through image-based leverage, reputational control, and platform migration rather than physical transportation or confinement. This Brief Report introduces the Youth Online Exploitation–Extremism Interface Framework, a conceptual-practice model with seven interacting domains: youth vulnerability, digital access points, coercive mechanisms, abuse-material production, group and status reinforcement, violence-aesthetic overlay, and response pathways. The domains are analytically separable but empirically entangled and recursive, so information in one domain can revise interpretation of another. The framework draws on 174 publicly documented incidents (2014 to 2026) in the PERIL Nihilistic Violent Extremism Tracker, serving as illustrative anchors rather than a representative sample for prevalence estimation. It supports identification, documentation, and survivor-centered referral across anti-trafficking, child protection, school safety, and survivor-serving settings. It is not a legal trafficking test, extremism classification, or risk-prediction instrument. Contribution to UN Sustainable Development Goals. This report supports UN Sustainable Development Goal 16, Target 16.2 (ending abuse, exploitation, trafficking, and violence against children). By giving anti-trafficking, child-protection, school-safety, and platform-safety practitioners a shared vocabulary for trafficking-adjacent online exploitation in violent online subcultures, the framework can improve early identification, documentation, and survivor-centered referrals. It also contributes to SDG 5 (Target 5.2, eliminating violence and exploitation against women and girls), foregrounding youth-facing sexual coercion and image-based sexual abuse as distinct yet co-occurring harms requiring a multi-system response. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
