AD  - University of Science and Technology of China, School of Computer Science and Technology, Hefei, China
AB  - Deep learning has achieved remarkable success across various fields, but its performance relies heavily on large-scale annotated data, limiting its application in real-world scenarios. Few-Shot Learning (FSL) provides an effective approach to address this data scarcity problem. Focusing on the key issues of feature extraction and feature representation in few-shot image classification tasks, this paper proposes a semantic-guided multi-vector feature representation method based on metric learning. In the feature extraction stage, an efficient Shuffle Attention (SA) mechanism is embedded into the ResNet-12 network to enhance the extraction of spatial and channel features from images. In terms of feature representation, semantic word vectors of categories are utilized to generate visual activation maps, which capture both the global and local features of the images. Furthermore, a base class prior knowledge transfer module is incorporated to transfer prior knowledge from base classes to novel classes, thereby enhancing the discriminability of novel class features. Experimental results on the miniImageNet, CUB, and CIFAR-FS datasets demonstrate that the proposed model improves classification accuracy in both 5-way 1-shot and 5-way 5-shot tasks, with average increases of 1.25% and 1.11%, respectively. © 2026 IEEE.
