AD  - Burapha University, Faculty of Informatics, Chonburi, 20131, Thailand
AB  - The increasing volume of municipal solid waste has created a demand for reliable waste- sorting systems with a low-cost sensing configuration. However, the RGB and RGB-depth (RGB-D) vision methods remain sensitive to illumination variation, occlusion, deformation, and visual similarity between recyclable materials, such as transparent plastic and glass. This study addresses these issues by presenting a sensing-to-sorting multimodal robotic waste-sorting pipeline integrating RGB-D vision, low-cost AS7265x visible–near-infrared spectral sensing, adaptive Bayesian fusion, and robotic pick-and-place execution. The vision module uses YOLOv8s instance segmentation for detection and localization, whereas the spectral module uses CatBoost to classify the 18-channel reflectance signatures. The fusion strategy combines vision and spectral probabilities using confidence-aware weighting, contact validation, uncertainty indicators, cross-modal agreement, class priors, and confusion-matrix likelihoods. The experiments used 2,000 RGB images with 24,848 annotated instances and 1,000 spectral samples across four classes: glass, metal, paper, and plastic. YOLOv8s achieved 0.960 mAP50 and 0.819 mAP50–95, whereas CatBoost achieved 73.0% accuracy. Real-world validation using a 600-object evaluation set tested under three lighting conditions showed that the proposed method achieved an accuracy and macro-F1 scores of 0.831 ± 0.023 and 0.829 ± 0.024, respectively, improving the average accuracy by approximately 10.9 and 11.8 percentage points over the vision- and spectral-only approaches, respectively. These results demonstrate improved robotic recyclable waste classification under practical lighting conditions with stable performance across lighting levels, thereby indicating the potential of the proposed system for small- and medium-scale recycling applications under laboratory conveyor-belt conditions. © 2013 IEEE.
