AD  - The University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston, McGovern Medical School, Houston, United States
AB  - Depression is a highly prevalent mental health disorder that significantly impairs quality of life and functional capacity. Accurate and timely identification is essential to support effective clinical intervention and long-term management. This study examines the potential of integrating multiple cohorts from the National Sleep Research Resource (NSRR) for depression detection based on multi-modal sleep electroencephalogram (EEG), demographic, and clinical data. We integrated three NSRR cohorts comprising 1,288 individuals (209 depressed and 1,079 non-depressed). Overnight sleep EEG recordings from C3/C4 electrode channels were used to characterize sleep-related neurophysiological patterns, together with demographic and clinical variables. To optimize predictive performance, feature selection was performed using Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator (LASSO), followed by evaluation using multiple classical machine learning and deep learning models. Predictive models were developed and evaluated using a rigorous validation framework to assess generalizability across heterogeneous datasets. Our best-performing models achieved an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of up to 0.688 with area under the precision-recall curve (AUPRC) up to 0.327 on the imbalanced cohort (positive-class prevalence ≈ 0.162), exceeding the random baseline expected under class imbalance. Although conservative relative to small-sample studies, these results reflect improved generalizability enabled by the large cohort size, multi-cohort design, and a strictly leakage-free evaluation in which feature selection is performed within each training fold. Overall, the findings demonstrate the feasibility of using multi-modal data from NSRR to support scalable and generalizable approaches for sleep EEG-based depression detection. © 2026 IEEE.
