AD  - University of Birmingham, Birmingham, United Kingdom
AD  - EMBL-EBI, Cambridge, United Kingdom
AD  - University of Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland
AB  - Shape is a fundamental property of cells and nuclei that informs on their function and state, and is one of the key readouts of 2D microscopy image data. Quantifying shape in an unbiased manner however remains challenging, especially in exploratory contexts where discovering phenotypes with as little prior as possible is highly desirable. In this work, we demonstrate the practical value of data-driven shape quantification methods on three publicly available microscopy image datasets of cells and nuclei. We compare the information that can be gathered from shape representations obtained with state-of-the-art self-supervised learning models and contrast it against the performance of the widely-used baseline consisting of describing shape with a collection of summary statistics. Our results identify the recently-published ShapeEmbed model as a powerful option, prompting us to propose a simpler variant, coined ShapeEmbedLite, which reduces the complexity of the approach without sacrificing performance and even achieve gains in some metrics. Through our experiments, we demonstrate that data-driven representations obtained through self-supervised learning can facilitate the visual and quantitative exploration of shape differences in 2D microscopy image data with minimal bias, and that the variations they capture align with true signal originating from different experimental conditions or biological states. These findings outline the promising outlook of such methods to drive biology discovery and to help identify rare or unknown shape phenotypes. © 2025 IEEE.
