AB  - Unlike traditional machine learning tasks under the independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) assumption, Covariate Shift (CS) tackles the learning problem where training and test (instance) marginal distributions differ while conditional distributions are the same. Further, datasets (or distributions) are often class-imbalanced in practice, where AUC is a widely adopted imbalance-aware evaluation metric. However, few works have investigated the problem of AUC-targeted imbalanced CS learning. In this work, we first provide a theoretical analysis of the ultimate learning goal, revealing key influencing factors. Inspired by the theoretical insights, we propose a new efficient triple-weighting method with univariate surrogate losses. Specifically, the proposed objective jointly incorporates three complementary weighting factors: the instance-level density ratio for correcting covariate shift, the class-wise average density ratios for aligning positive and negative sample contributions under the test distribution, and the imbalance-aware degree for alleviating class-prior bias. This design enables the model to optimize an AUC-oriented objective under the target distribution while avoiding the high computational cost of pairwise AUC losses. The proposed framework can be naturally instantiated with density-ratio-based covariate shift methods, such as static importance weighting and dynamic importance weighting. Theoretically, we provide favorable consistency and generalization guarantees. Finally, experimental results illustrate its effectiveness and robustness compared with related baselines. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
