TI  - Unraveling the efficiency-equity trade-off in urban heat island mitigation by blue–green–grey space optimization in China
PY  - 2026
T2  - Urban Climate
VL  - 68
C7  - 103041
DO  - 10.1016/j.uclim.2026.103041
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044034978?origin=resultslist
AD  - Institute of Tibetan Plateau Research, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100101, China
AD  - University of Chinese Academy Sciences, Beijing, 100049, China
AB  - Rapid urbanization has shifted the urban climate management paradigm from passive expansion control to the active optimization of existing biophysical properties. However, conventional mitigation frameworks often overlook the physical interdependence of urban surfaces and the inequity inherent in cooling resource distribution. This study integrates interpretable machine learning to quantify seasonal thermal contributions and a novel 3D Convex Hull model to determine the physically constrained mitigation ceilings of Blue–Green–Grey (BGG) spaces across 199 major prefecture-level cities in China (2000–2024) at a 1-km spatial resolution. The proposed method achieved good random hold-out performance (R2 = 0.83) and revealed a distinct spatiotemporal differentiation of cooling mechanisms: Green space is statistically associated with the largest share of model-attributed cooling and exhibits a clear phenological curve, especially in the ecologically restored Loess Plateau. Blue space provides a unique dual-seasonal thermal buffer, peaking in both summer and winter. Grey space contribution is largely radiation-dependent, becoming highly pronounced in summer. Overall, BGG optimization can achieve a local cooling capacity of 1.01–5.46 °C, with a critical efficiency-equity trade-off under various intervention scenarios. While maximizing cooling efficiency yields a superior mitigation capacity (2.23 °C), it exacerbates spatial inequality. Conversely, cooling footprint expansion (1.83 °C) enhances spatial equity by reducing the Gini index by 0.3. These findings provide practical support for climate-resilient urban development. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
