AB  - Based on the panel data of China and RCEP partner countries from 2007 to 2022, this paper constructs a digital economy measurement system by employing principal component analysis, a core dimensionality reduction algorithm in machine learning, to quantify the level of digital economy development. The extended trade gravity model is used to analyse the impact of digital economy development on China's cultural product exports. The study leverages core computer technologies, including big data analytics for processing trade and consumption data, and incorporates discussions on the roles of AI-driven content adaptation and blockchain-based transaction systems. The study finds that: the development of the digital economy in RCEP partner countries has a significant positive effect on China's cultural product exports. This underscores the critical role of underlying digital infrastructure and data-driven decision-making. The heterogeneity test shows that the improvement of digital economy level in developing countries has a more prominent role in promoting China's cultural product exports, suggesting that algorithm and platform design should consider regional disparities. Accordingly, it puts forward policy suggestions such as strengthening intelligent digital infrastructure and AI-powered content adaptation tools, establishing blockchain-based copyright and transaction mechanisms, focusing on digital cultural exports to RCEP developing country markets, participating in formulating international technical standards for digital cultural assets, and building a national-level cloud-native digital cultural export platform, so as to promote the high-quality development of China's cultural product export trade and provide insights for the development of computational economic models. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
