AB  - Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) demonstrate powerful capabilities in graph representation learning tasks. However, real-world graphs are often perturbed and come with noisy labels, resulting in poor performance of GNNs in this scenario. To bridge this gap, we study a novel problem of developing robust graph adversarial defense on perturbed graphs with noisy labels, and propose a graph adversarial defense named Comprehensive AdversaRial Defense (CARD). Initially, we utilize unsupervised singular value decomposition to separate the graph, preserving its main portion while isolating the mixed effects of perturbed graphs and noisy labels. Next, we employ an edge repairer to further restore the graph, thereby enhancing the robustness of GNNs against perturbed graphs. Subsequently, we perform fine-grained dynamic weighting of node-level losses during the training phase to prevent overfitting, and employ label alignment to address the significant label shift issue, thereby reducing the impact of noisy labels. Ultimately, we organize sufficient experiments under four benchmark datasets, four types of perturbed graphs, and two types of noisy labels. The empirical results validate the superior performance of CARD on the above adversarial scenarios. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
