TI  - Tooth segmentation in mixed dentition CBCT via a three-stage class-prior and morphology-aware framework
PY  - 2026
T2  - Neurocomputing
VL  - 701
C7  - 134513
DO  - 10.1016/j.neucom.2026.134513
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044985891?origin=resultslist
AD  - College of Computer Science, Sichuan University, Chengdu, 610065, China
AD  - Department of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery, West China Hospital of Stomatology, Sichuan University, Chengdu, 610041, China
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Hong Kong, 999077, China
AD  - School of Artificial Intelligence, Sichuan University, Chengdu, 610065, China
AB  - Accurate three-dimensional (3D) tooth segmentation in pediatric cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) scans remains challenging during the mixed dentition stage due to its complex anatomical characteristics. Existing methods designed for permanent dentition often fail to generalize effectively, primarily owing to the increased number of teeth, crowding, intricate spatial arrangements, and significant morphological variations inherent to mixed dentition. To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel three-stage segmentation framework that incorporates class-prior guidance and morphology-aware refinement. In the first stage, a 3D morphological constraint graph network (MCG-Net) is introduced, integrating a class prior constraint graph module (CPCGM) to dynamically model inter-class dependencies based on spatial and developmental priors of mixed dentition, and a morphological constraint module (MCM) to enhance structural precision through boundary-aware supervision. In the second stage, a 3D segmentation and classification refinement network (SCR-Net) is developed to jointly refine single-tooth segmentation and adjust the first-stage class predictions via multi-task learning, thereby mitigating the effects of tooth crowding and morphological variability. In the final stage, a class-weighted strategy based on voxel count is designed to reduce class conflicts in overlapping regions, improving segmentation accuracy for smaller teeth. The proposed framework is validated on a self-constructed mixed dentition dataset and a publicly available permanent dentition dataset. Quantitative and qualitative results demonstrate that our method achieves superior segmentation accuracy and robustness for mixed dentition, while maintaining competitive performance on permanent dentition. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
