AB  - Cancer is still a major global health issue and one of the leading causes of death globally. Despite significant progress in the field of machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) in the medical field and especially in cancer research, uncertainty quantification has been largely under-investigated, resulting in the development of decision systems with questionable reliability and confidence, which are essential for effective integration and adoption in a clinical context. In this study, we evaluate and discuss five uncertainty quantification (UQ) methods: Single Deterministic, Monte Carlo Dropout (MCD), Bayesian Neural Network based on Variational Inference (BNN), Deep Ensemble (DE), and Bootstrap Ensemble (BG) for the identification of breast cancer molecular subtype using a deep neural network (DNN). These empirical evaluations were based, in addition to the classical classification performance criteria such as accuracy, recall, precision and f1 score, on other advanced criteria related to sensibility, interpretability, robustness, and out-of-distribution (OOD) detection. Moreover, we introduce and evaluate new algorithms for synthetic OOD data generation, crucial for assessing the reliability of quantified uncertainties. Results indicated that Ensemble-based UQ methods generally provided more robust uncertainty estimates and performed best in terms of sensitivity. Stochastic methods, on the other hand, achieved superior calibration, with Bayesian Neural Networks in particular demonstrating strong robustness to adversarial attacks, an attribute that may enhance the generalization capacity of classifiers. Overall, we found that uncertainty estimates not only provide reliable confidence measures but also enhance the transparency of model predictions, supporting safer clinical deployment. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
