AD  - School of Energy and Safety Engineering, Tianjin Chengjian University, Tianjin, China
AB  - This chapter comprehensively examines non-contact monitoring technologies for heating, ventilation, and air conditioning (HVAC) systems, focusing on infrared thermography and computer vision (CV) applications. Infrared thermography (IRT) enables precise fault detection in mechanical, electrical, and HVAC equipment by capturing temperature anomalies, with advancements in image enhancement algorithms improving visualization and diagnostic accuracy. CV techniques automate frost/dew observation and enhance defrost control, utilizing machine learning to quantify frost thickness, coverage, and fractal dimension for optimized defrost timing. In condensation heat transfer measurement, CV technology integrates droplet dynamics with deep learning to predict heat transfer performance, reducing experimental errors via multi-angle imaging and extended video analysis. Applications span energy infrastructure monitoring and equipment operation performance assessment. Future directions emphasize multi-technology integration (IRT and CV and artificial intelligence), dynamic droplet feature extraction, and universal condensation models to minimize energy consumption and enhance system reliability. These innovations collectively advance intelligent HVAC management, supporting energy efficiency and sustainability across built environmental domains. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Bin Yang, Zhihan Lyu, Xiaogang Cheng, and Yihang Liu; individual chapters, the contributors.
