UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85207862564?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Computer Sciences, University of Wisconsin-Madison, United States
AD  - Department of Statistics, University of Wisconsin-Madison, United States
AD  - Department of Materials Sciences and Engineering, University of Wisconsin-Madison, United States
AB  - A method is proposed to accelerate ensemble uncertainty quantification methods by replacing the ensemble method inference with a single model. An ensemble of N models is approximately N times more computationally demanding compared to a similar complexity single model when it is used for inference. We focus on regression models for material properties. Three material data sets were studied which include property data related to dilute solute diffusion, perovskite stability, and super conduction critical temperatures. We generated uncertainties from calibrated ensemble models for the aforementioned datasets. We explored fitting a single model to replace ensemble uncertainty estimates, which allows us to estimate uncertainties without the need for a full ensemble. Our approach is based on three models: Model A for predictive accuracy, Model AE for ensemble-based uncertainty prediction, and Model B, fit to data from Model AE, to be used for predicting the values of AE but with only one model evaluation. Model B leverages synthetic data augmentation to estimate uncertainties efficiently. This approach offers a highly flexible method of uncertainty quantification that can approximate that of ensemble methods but only requires a single extra model evaluation over Model A during inference. We evaluate the performance of this approach on a set of problems in materials sciences using modest-sized datasets with fewer than 100 features, demonstrating its potential generalizability to similar models and datasets. © 2024 Elsevier B.V.
