AB  - The introduction of voxel-based morphometry (VBM) has profoundly impacted neuroimaging, offering a robust and objective approach to analyze brain structure using MRI. This study presents a customized pipeline for VBM methodology and its applications in both healthy elderly individuals and patients with Parkinson's disease (PD). The VBM process mainly involves spatial normalization to a standard template, segmentation into gray matter (GM) and white matter (WM), smoothing, and rigorous statistical analysis. VBM has proven invaluable in detecting subtle brain structural changes in various neurological and psychiatric conditions, as well as identifying gross structural abnormalities. The study highlights VBM's crucial role in advancing neuroimaging research, making it an indispensable tool in the field. While VBM has significantly advanced our understanding of brain morphology, careful attention to the algorithmic implementation is essential to ensure the accuracy of conclusions about anatomical differences related to disease states. This study underscores that combining VBM with complementary neuroimaging techniques can greatly enhance our understanding of normal brain processes and improve comparisons between patient and control groups. This study deals with 12 healthy controls (HCs) and 30 PD patients. It is concluded that a more effective biomarker could be prepared with the help of suggested pipeline of automated VBM without custom templates and custom tissue class priors.  © 2024 IEEE.
