TI  - Adversarial Open-Set Domain Adaptation for Cross-Condition Fault Diagnosis of Marine Gearboxes
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Conference on Electronic Measurement and Instruments
IS  - 2025
SP  - 429
EP  - 434
DO  - 10.1109/ICEMI66537.2025.11306579
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034144642?origin=resultslist
AD  - Yantai Port Tow Age Co., Ltd, Shandong Port Group, Yantai, China
AB  - The gearbox is a critical component of the ship propulsion system, and its health directly impacts operational safety and reliability. Conventional deep-learning-based diagnostic methods, however, struggle under cross-condition scenarios due to domain shift and the presence of unseen fault types. To address these challenges, we propose an Adversarial Open-Set Domain Adaptation (AOSDA) framework. AOSDA employs a dual-path adversarial training scheme: one path constructs and refines decision boundaries for known faults in both source and target domains, driving target-domain features into alignment; the other path actively pushes unknown-fault features away from these boundaries. We further incorporate evidential deep learning to quantify domain uncertainty and adaptively adjust the threshold for unknown-class detection, thereby enhancing robustness in high-noise environments. A ResNet-based time-frequency feature extractor captures both local and global signal characteristics. We evaluate AOSDA on six cross-condition transfer tasks drawn from two public bearing and gearbox vibration datasets, systematically comparing against four state- of- the- art baselines. Results show that AOSDA achieves the highest classification accuracy for both known and unknown faults (93.2% and 91.6%, respectively). t-SNE visualizations illustrate its synergistic effect on feature alignment and unknown-fault separation, and confusion-matrix analysis confirms high recall for unknown faults alongside high precision for known faults. The proposed method thus offers an effective solution for intelligent operation and maintenance of marine gearboxes under dynamic conditions and open set fault scenarios. © 2025 IEEE.
