AB  - The International Classification of Diseases (ICD) has been adopted worldwide in the healthcare domain, e.g. to summarize the key information in clinical documents. Since manual ICD coding is very expensive, time-consuming, and error-prone, deep learning algorithms have been proposed to automate this task. However, the final goal of ICD coding often lays not in determining the codes associated with individual documents, but instead in quantifying the prevalence of each code within sets of documents. In this work, we experimentally assess different quantification methods in connection to ICD coding, including a simple learning-based approach that leverages associations between the codes, in order to predict their relative frequencies more accurately. Experiments show that the proposed approach can effectively explore existing associations between ICD codes, improving the quantification performance over baseline methods that deal with each code independently. © 2023, The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.
