AB  - With the advancement of 5G and artificial intelligence technologies, unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) has emerged as crucial elements in modern warfare. However, their small size and slow speed make them difficult to detect and easily mistaken for birds or other flying objects, posing challenges for defenders to respond promptly. Moreover, existing methods for evaluating UAV threats heavily rely on subjective expert scoring, leading to inefficiency, uncertainty, and ambiguity. In this paper, we propose a comprehensive approach for UAV swarm threat perception and evaluation in integrated air-ground networks. By integrating historical observation data, we establish an indicator quantification method to evaluate the perception of UAV swarm threats. Initially, we employ convolutional neural networks for intent recognition, allowing for coarse-grained classification of enemy UAVs. Subsequently, we utilize transfer learning to in-corporate the trained intent recognition model into the threat evaluation framework, significantly enhancing the efficiency and accuracy of UAV threat evaluation. Experimental results confirm the superior performance of our UAV swarm threat perception and evaluation model compared to existing approaches.  © 2023 IEEE.
