AB  - Reliable classification of heartbeat sounds is crucial for computer-aided diagnosis in cardiology. This paper presents a deep learning-based framework enhanced with Monte Carlo Dropout (MCD) to estimate predictive uncertainty - an oftenoverlooked aspect in medical AI systems. We evaluate three Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architectures: a custom Swish-activated CNN, a Two-Stream CNN combining MFCC and Mel-spectrogram features, and a Two-Stream FractalNet. While the Swish CNN attained the highest classification accuracy 85.00%, the Two-Stream CNN demonstrated the most favorable uncertainty profile, with lower predictive entropy and better calibration. These results highlight the importance of not only achieving high accuracy but also understanding model confidence, especially in clinical decision-making scenarios. The use of Monte Carlo Dropout provides deeper insight into model behavior, making it a valuable tool for enhancing the safety and reliability of diagnostic systems. Future work will focus on expanding the dataset, integrating advanced uncertainty quantification methods, and enabling real-time deployment in medical settings. © 2025 IEEE.
