AB  - Automated blood cell analysis plays a crucial role in early disease diagnosis. Traditional microscopic examination remains labor-intensive and prone to subjective variability. In this study, we have proposed an enhanced YOLOv10-based detection framework integrated with Efficient Channel Attention (ECA) to classify and count red blood cells (RBCs), white blood cells (WBCs), and platelets from peripheral blood smear images. The ECA mechanism improves channel-wise feature selection without introducing computational overhead, enabling more accurate discrimination of morphologically similar cell types. Additionally, we have introduced an improved preprocessing pipeline, Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization (CLAHE) that enhances local contrast in an image along with other techniques. The final model achieves an accuracy of approximately 97.00% across all classes, demonstrating superior robustness in both detection and categorization. These results highlight the potential of attention-enhanced lightweight architectures for clinical hematological analysis and pave the way for reliable and rapid diagnostic support.  © 2025 IEEE.
