AD  - Institut für Mikrobiologie und Tierseuchen
AD  - Umweltbundesamt, FG Mikrobiologische Risiken
AB  - The use of antibiotics in companion animals is a key One Health concern, as antimicrobial-resistant bacteria can be transmitted between animals and humans, limiting treatment options in both veterinary and human medicine. This study aimed to assess whether routine data from veterinary practices are suitable for establishing an antibiotic monitoring system for dogs, cats, and horses. We analyzed over 3.7 million consultation records from 106 practices (2018–2023) and compared three quantification methods: (a) the proportion of consultations involving antibiotic treatment, (b) the number of Used Daily Doses (nUDD), and (c) the number of Defined Daily Doses (nDDD). Cats had the highest median proportion of antibiotic-treated consultations, while horses had the lowest. Overall, practice classifications into low, medium, or high antibiotic use were largely consistent across the three indicators. The nDDD could be calculated for most cases but required numerous assumptions regarding dosage and standard animal weights. The nUDD relied on standard treatment durations due to the lack of documented therapy lengths. In contrast, the proportion of antibiotic-treated consultations was fully calculable from routine data and closely aligned with the other indicators. Given its simplicity, transparency, and full data availability, this indicator appears most suitable – at least during the implementation phase of a monitoring system. However, for comprehensive surveillance and antimicrobial stewardship, future systems should also integrate diagnostic information and antimicrobial classes to support prudent use and resistance prevention more effectively. © 2025 Schlütersche Fachmedien GmbH, ein Unternehmen der Schlüterschen Mediengruppe
