TI  - Emissions Optimization Platform to Quantify and Reduce Rig-Based Emissions
PY  - 2023
T2  - Society of Petroleum Engineers - ADIPEC, ADIP 2023
DO  - 10.2118/215971-MS
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85176799572?origin=resultslist
AD  - Baker Hughes, Dhahran, Saudi Arabia
AB  - Reducing emissions at each step of the Oil & Gas Value chain is needed to help meet the global goal of keeping the temperature increase below 1.5C° of preindustrial level. This paper presents a method to calculate emissions and optimize operations to reduce emissions at the well planning stage. Three specific rigs were chosen and the data from their operations and the diesel consumption over the last 18 months (about 1 and a half years) was used to build a model that links specific operation on the rig with its associated emissions. Part of the data was used for model building while the rest was used for model verification. The energy consumption of each piece of equipment on the rig was also used in the calculations to document their wattage. An optimization tool is developed to help optimize rig operations for emissions reduction by choosing the proper rig and the rest of the equipment. The diesel usage data was lower resolution (one data points per day) compared to the operations (hourly). So, the model development was particularly challenging. It was found that there is considerable room for energy optimization especially with regards to the use of diesel generators. A considerable amount of excess energy is produced which gets wasted. Also, the cementing and casing has the greatest impact on the emissions as both materials emit a huge amount of carbon. Optimization of the emissions on the well planning stage is much more effective than emission reduction strategies post planning as the room for optimization is significantly less. Our approach is based on state-of-the-art deep reinforcement learning (DRL) and data science techniques that have been proven to work on complex systems that have different inputs. DRL trains an algorithm with a reward system that supplies positive and negative feedback (degree of emissions) to an Artificial Intelligence (AI) agent for performing actions. These actions are the various strategies in rig operations the agent takes to reduce emissions, the lower the emissions the higher the rewards for the agent. In iterative process the agent narrows down the best policy (plan) for each rig that lowers the emission. The use of data science and machine learning in well planning for emissions reduction is a novel approach with expert feedback being constant in our methods. Using DRL (deep reinforcement learning) to quantify emissions in well planning is a revolutionary technique. It is the first time in the industry that DRL is being used to assist in emissions reductions from upstream activities. We bring technology that has redefined the capabilities of AI in a variety of applications, including reservoir optimization and complex decision-making systems. © 2023, Society of Petroleum Engineers.
