AB  - This paper examines the geographical pattern of the prevalence of enterobiasis among children in Estonian counties, and methods for risk modelling. The methodological questions were as follows:(1)Is the prevalence of enterobiasis related to geographical region and settlement size?(2)Is the reliability of risk estimations related to the modelling method and sample size? Case-based predictions were more liable to result in over fitting in the case of small training samples than the classification tree models. The spatial autocorrelation of the prevalence was significant up to a distance of 20 km. The main reason for the differences in the predictions might simply be the regional differences in the prevalence. © 2009 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
